window.docContents[81].resolve({"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L3-___-The-body-opens-up/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L3-___-The-body-opens-up--Synopsis--Verifiable-properties":{"contents":"* Body A's centre of mass is the mass-weighted mean of its halves' positions: halves of mass 3 and 5 at positions 0 and 10 give 25/4, not the midpoint 5 (theorem) HCP.L3_TheBodyOpens#center_of_mass_mean.* PhysLib's centerOfMass of a body with zero mass is the origin, because 1/0 = 0 in Lean; body A's carving comes with a proof that its total mass is nonzero (theorem) HCP.L3_TheBodyOpens#massless_center_of_mass.* Angular velocity is equal on both halves and is not a sum over them, and assemble of angular velocity over the halves fails: there is no Assembles rigidBodyS angularVelocityK instance (refused) HCP.L3_TheBodyOpens#angular_velocity_intensive.* rotationalContraction of the body-frame inertia with the lab-frame ω fails with a type mismatch between InFrame lab and InFrame body (refused) HCP.L3_TheBodyOpens#framed_rotational_energy.* With the body-frame ω, the kinded rotational energy equals PhysLib's rotational kinetic energy (theorem) HCP.L3_TheBodyOpens#framed_rotational_energy.* A homogeneous solid sphere of mass m and radius R has moment of inertia (2/5) m R² (theorem) HCP.L3_TheBodyOpens#solid_sphere.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L3 — The body opens up\u0009Synopsis","header":"5.4.1.3. Verifiable properties","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L3-___-The-body-opens-up/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L3-___-The-body-opens-up--Synopsis--Verifiable-properties"}});